       "While forming class intervals, the exclusive (10–20, 20–30) or inclusive (10–19, 20–29) method is used.",
       "In the exclusive method the upper limit goes to the next class; in inclusive both limits stay in the same class.",
       "Types of class limits — class limits (10–20) and true class limits (9.5–19.5, adjusted in inclusive).",
       "Mid-value = (lower limit + upper limit) ÷ 2 — e.g. the mid-value of 10–20 is 15.",
       "Frequency is the number telling how many times a value occurs; cumulative frequency is progressively added.",
       "A frequency distribution table has columns for class, tally marks, frequency and cumulative frequency.",
       "'Less than' and 'more than' cumulative frequency distributions are prepared.",
       "Equal class intervals ease comparison and diagramming; starting the first class at 0 is best.",
